  "notes": "Original Colorado One (2012) marked the 5,000th 737 built (N230WN). Drawn from the only current photo, N8690A from the right in cloud (Dec 2025). The left side is taken from the previous Colorado One, N230WN (July 2024), where the C opens aft: the flag reads the same way on both sides rather than mirrored. Not confirmed on N8690A's left side. The crown's blue fades from view aft of the wing in the 2025 photo (cloud); drawn ending there, which may be the light. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on the same photo, flipped and fitted to the measured 737-800 shape: the crown's lower edge runs under the cockpit windows (y about 0.07 at the nose, not 0.65) and rises to about 0.47 behind the C; the belly's upper edge sits about y −0.5 (lower than first drawn) and rises aft to pass under the aft door; the C and the disc redrawn as true circles (2.9 m and 1.4 m radius on the side, centred about 10.8 m back), all smooth; the fin's bands re-measured (as shares of the photo's chord: the measured shape's fin is narrower than the photo's). 'Colorado One' is two weights, so it's a pair of titles per side; it sits about y −0.1, well under the cockpit windows. The aft belly and tail cone are in cloud: their edge there is the least certain. Nose re-measured 2026-11-15 (probe): the crown's blue comes down to the nose tip itself and its edge rises to about y 0.19 just ahead of the cockpit windows; the lower nose and radome's underside are white, the belly's blue starting at the belly behind the nose gear (x about 0.06) and rising to y −0.54 by x 0.2."
}
